w: As_ Rod-dy Mc-Cor-ley_ goes to die on the Bridge of Toome to-day.
%
W:Up the narrow street he stepped, smiling proud and young.
W:About the hemp-rope on his neck the golden ringlets clung.
W:But there never a tear in his blue eyes, both glad and bright are they,
W:As young Roddy McCorley goes to die on the Bridge of Toome today
W:
W:When he last stepped up that street, his shining pike in hand,
W:Behind him marched in grim array a stalwart earnest band.
W:For Antrim town! For Antrim town! He led them to the fray,
W:As young Roddy McCorley goes to die on the Bridge of Toome today
W:
W:There is never a one of all your dead more bravely fell in fray,
W:Than he who marches to his fate on the Bridge of Toome today.
W:True to the last, true to the last, he treads the uward way,
W:And young Roddy McCorley goes to die on the Bridge of Toome today

